        "Dog DNA testing grew from a party trick - what breeds is my rescue? - into a genuine health product, and the writing about it never caught up: most of what owners find is either brand copy promising certainty or dismissals that treat the whole category as astrology with a swab. Both are wrong, and both leave the actual questions unanswered. This guide is the complete, honest map. It covers what a dog DNA test actually measures and how a cheek swab becomes a report; what breed ancestry results mean and the precise sense in which they are estimates; what a genetic health flag is and - just as important - what it is not; the newer branches of the category, from age estimation to gut health; who the companies are, how they differ, and how we rank them; what testing costs, with the one brand's pricing we have verified against its own store; and the practice - when to test, how to swab, what to do with a scary result, how results touch insurance and breed-restriction questions - that decides whether the kit was worth buying. Three commitments shape everything below, because they shape this whole site. First, numbers are verified or they are absent: the prices and panel figures in this guide are the Embark numbers we checked against the brand's own store in September 2026, and where we have not verified a figure - Wisdom Panel's pricing, for instance - we say 'we haven't verified this yet' instead of guessing. Second, no invented accuracy claims: no regulator certifies breed-percentage accuracy in consumer pet genetics, no honest guide prints a precision number for it, and the section on accuracy explains what you can rely on instead. Third, the vet outranks us: a health flag belongs in a conversation at the clinic, not in a panic at midnight, and every health section below says so. Read it straight through or jump to your question - each section stands alone on purpose.",
