        "The pipeline is the same at every serious brand: swab, lab, genotyping, algorithms, report. The swab collects cheek cells, which carry the same DNA as every other cell in the dog; you rub the brush against the inside of the cheek, air-dry or stabilize it per the kit's instructions, activate the kit online so the lab can tie the sample to your dog's profile, and mail it in. At the lab, DNA is extracted and run on a genotyping array - a chip that reads the genome at a large set of pre-selected positions chosen because they are informative: positions that differ between breeds, positions inside known disease-associated variants, positions that control visible traits. This is the step that separates serious tests from cheap ones - the size and design of the array bounds everything the report can say. Then two different kinds of analysis run on the same data. Health and trait results are close to direct lookups: the variant behind a given condition or coat color either is present in your dog's genotype or is not, in one copy or two - which is why these results are the most reliable layer of the report. Breed ancestry is statistical inference: an algorithm searches for the combination of reference-breed segments that best explains your dog's chromosomes, which is why ancestry comes with soft edges - it is an estimate whose quality depends on the reference database and the algorithm, not a direct observation. The report wraps it all in plain language: a breed breakdown with percentages, trait explanations, and health results usually sorted into clear categories - at risk, carrier, clear - with guidance written to be shared with your vet. Timelines run in weeks, varying by brand and season. The one thing the pipeline cannot do is read what is not on the array: a condition with no known genetic marker, a breed absent from the reference panel - the report is silent about both, which is why 'clear' means 'clear of what we tested', never 'healthy'.",
