        "Breed ancestry is reconstruction, not recognition: no gene says 'beagle', so the algorithm infers which mixture of reference breeds best explains the patterns in your dog's genome. The mechanics matter because they explain every oddity owners meet in their results. Each breed, being a closed breeding population, carries characteristic patterns - combinations of variants at far higher frequency inside the breed than outside it. The testing company maintains a reference panel: genotypes from confirmed purebred dogs across hundreds of breeds - 400+ breeds and varieties at Embark on the panel we verified. The algorithm walks your dog's chromosomes segment by segment, asking which reference breed each stretch most resembles, then assembles the percentage breakdown that best explains the whole. Three practical consequences follow. First, the reference panel bounds the answer: a breed, landrace or regional population not well represented in the panel cannot be named, and the algorithm will describe those segments using the nearest breeds it does know - one honest reason results differ between brands. Second, generations blur the signal: a grandparent's breed arrives in large, confident chunks, but five generations back the inherited segments are short and fragmentary, which is why small trailing percentages are the fuzziest part of any breakdown and why 'supermutt'-style catch-all labels exist and are honest. Third, village dogs and ancient mixed populations are not failures of the test - a dog descended from generations of free-breeding carries genuinely no tidy breed recipe, and a good report says so rather than inventing one. None of this makes breed results unreliable; it makes them estimates with a known shape - strong on the major components, soft on the trace ones. Read the top of the breakdown with confidence and the tail with a smile, and if two brands disagree about the trace percentages, you have learned more about reference panels than about your dog."