        "Early - the sample is cheek cells and the genotype is fixed from birth, so the practical constraints are swab logistics rather than biology: most brands support testing as soon as a puppy is weaned and can be swabbed cleanly, and the information arrives at the moment of maximum usefulness. Why early testing is the high-value play: every actionable layer of the report compounds with lead time. A drug-sensitivity flag discovered at twelve weeks informs the very first anesthesia event - often the spay or neuter - rather than being discovered after it. Size genetics answers the how-big question while crates, food plans and apartment decisions are still being made. Risk flags for adult-onset conditions buy years of quiet monitoring. And the breed breakdown lands while training and socialization approaches are still forming, when knowing the engine under the hood - herding drive, scent obsession, guarding tendencies in the ancestry - can usefully shape what you practice early. The swab logistics that actually matter: cheek cells need to be your puppy's alone, so follow the kit's instructions about separating the swab from recent nursing, shared water bowls and litter-mates' enthusiastic face-licking - cross-contamination from another dog's saliva is the one realistic way to spoil a sample, and it is entirely avoidable with a short wait after meals and a minute of isolation. (The practical swab section later covers technique for wiggly subjects.) One honest caution about early results: a puppy's report is a genetic document, not a crystal ball - size estimates carry ranges, risk flags carry penetrance, and the dog's actual adult temperament will be built as much by the next year of socialization as by anything in the ancestry. Test early for the lead time, hold the predictions loosely, and let the report be what it is at its best: the baseline document in a brand-new medical file, filed before there is any history to confound it.",
