        "If you are hoping a DNA test will settle a breed-restriction question - housing rules, local regulations, airline and insurer breed lists - our honest caution is that this road disappoints in both directions: the test is not designed as a legal instrument, and introducing its results into a restriction dispute can hurt as easily as help. Why the fit is poor, structurally. Breed-restriction rules are written around breed labels; DNA results are statistical ancestry estimates with soft edges - the accuracy section explained why trace percentages are fuzzy and why brands can disagree on the tail of a breakdown. A rule that asks 'is this dog breed X?' and a report that answers 'estimated twelve percent breed X, with error bars' are not speaking the same language, and how any given landlord, regulator or adjudicator maps one onto the other is unpredictable. The practical asymmetry owners should see before swabbing for this purpose: a result showing none of the restricted breed may or may not persuade a decision-maker who was going on looks - visual breed judgment is exactly what these rules usually run on, and nothing obliges a landlord to prefer your genetics to their eyes; while a result showing some percentage of a restricted breed creates a document that now exists, says so, and cannot be unknown. Testing a dog that currently passes visual muster, specifically to generate paperwork for a dispute, can manufacture the very problem it hoped to dismiss. What we can constructively say: know the actual wording of any rule you live under - some name breeds, some name appearance, some defer to specific documentation, and the wording decides whether any genetic evidence is even relevant; have that conversation before generating evidence, ideally with advice appropriate to the stakes (for consequential disputes, that means someone qualified in your jurisdiction, not a pet website - ours included); and if you test, test for the reasons the product is good at - health flags, identity, care planning - and let that be why the report exists. This guide's job is the honest map, and on this question the honest map says: proceed with eyes open.",
